  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The performance and scheduling outcomes are evaluated through simulated computational experiments rather than a real-world physical deployment.",
      "what": "This study models and solves a scheduling problem for point-to-point aerial transport using collective drones, where multiple smaller drones cooperate to carry heavy, bulky loads. The authors formulate the scheduling problem to minimize makespan while ensuring drone capacities meet job weights and allow for transit times between tasks, evaluating their decomposition-based solution methods and fleet compositions through a computational study.",
      "why": "It establishes a scheduling framework for a new logistics paradigm where drone fleets are dynamically coupled to transport heavy goods, moving beyond the traditional single-drone, lightweight delivery model."
    }
